ers/enoperm","html_url":"https://github.com/enoperm","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enoperm/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enoperm/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enoperm/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enoperm/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enoperm/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enoperm/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enoperm/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enoperm/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/enoperm/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2022-01-30T14:32:01Z","updated_at":"2022-01-30T14:32:01Z","body":"> @enoperm, could you help me test this? It seem to work for my sqlite setup, and I think this is needed as 0.13.0-beta2.\r\n\r\nI too, have a sqlite setup, so if that works for you I expect no differences here. My current in-use database is old enough to have an `ip_address` field, but it is all empty as all current nodes in it have joined after the change to `ip_addresses`, so if anything, this would break *that* setup, I'll have to drop that column before I upgrade to this version if I do not want lose all my addresses. :)\r\n\r\nI could try configuring an older 0.11 or 0.12 build with postgres and upgrade that, in case you were looking to confirm behaviour on such a setup.\r\n\r\nAt any rate, the code change itself looks good, assuming it does not fail when the column does not exists anymore (ie. subsequent startups), but I suppose that's why the return value is ignored.","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/juanfont/headscale/issues/comments/1025155903/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null,"event"